WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.941
Same anatomy, different products.

2
00:00:02.221 --> 00:00:12.845
Every mainstream coding agent has the same five parts: an instruction file the model reads, a tool set, an approval system, some form of sandbox, and an undo story.

3
00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:17.349
Here is how the official docs describe each product at the time of writing.

4
00:00:17.629 --> 00:00:21.597
Treat the details as fast-moving; the anatomy is the stable part.

5
00:00:21.877 --> 00:00:24.011
OpenAI Codex reads AGENTS.

6
00:00:24.291 --> 00:00:29.688
md files, walking from the project root down to the current directory (with an AGENTS.

7
00:00:29.968 --> 00:00:30.843
override.

8
00:00:31.123 --> 00:00:35.667
md option and a default 32 KiB cap on combined instructions).

9
00:00:35.947 --> 00:00:48.491
Its security docs separate two controls: a sandbox mode ( read-only, workspace-write, danger-full-access ) that sets what is technically possible, and an approval policy that sets when it must ask.

10
00:00:48.771 --> 00:00:57.347
Network access is off by default, and web search defaults to a cached index, with the docs telling you to treat web results as untrusted.

11
00:00:57.627 --> 00:00:59.717
Gemini CLI reads GEMINI.

12
00:00:59.997 --> 00:01:07.144
md files (global, workspace, and subdirectory files loaded just in time) and can be told to read AGENTS.

13
00:01:07.424 --> 00:01:08.427
md too.

14
00:01:08.707 --> 00:01:18.456
Its tools include read_file, write_file, replace, glob, grep_search, run_shell_command, and web_fetch, and the shell and write tools require confirmation.

15
00:01:18.736 --> 00:01:32.880
Note the status change: the Gemini CLI docs carry a banner saying that for unpaid-tier and Google One users, Gemini CLI was replaced by Antigravity CLI on June 18, 2026.

16
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:39.773
Antigravity’s migration guide says it keeps Gemini CLI’s core constructs and reads both GEMINI.

17
00:01:40.053 --> 00:01:41.376
md and AGENTS.

18
00:01:41.656 --> 00:01:42.467
md.

19
00:01:42.747 --> 00:01:44.688
GitHub Copilot has two shapes.

20
00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:55.464
In VS Code, agents run locally with tools, approvals you configure, and diffs to review; the docs note that stopping a request doesn’t undo completed actions.

21
00:01:55.744 --> 00:02:08.352
On GitHub, the cloud agent works in a GitHub Actions environment or a cloud sandbox, on one branch at a time, opening one pull request per task, with Actions sessions capped at 59 minutes.

22
00:02:08.632 --> 00:02:09.805
Copilot reads.

23
00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:12.176
github/copilot-instructions.

24
00:02:12.456 --> 00:02:16.296
md, path-specific instruction files, and the nearest AGENTS.

25
00:02:16.576 --> 00:02:17.387
md.

26
00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:27.971
Cursor’s Agent combines search, edit, and terminal tools, lets you choose the model per task, and saves local checkpoints before significant changes, separate from git.
